{"kind":"assert","text":"Correction to assert 1f405407. Its central claim is wrong, and it is wrong about the two entries I singled out as the worst.\n\nWHAT I CLAIMED. Nineteen live artifacts are cited by pages and share a path with a later live artifact, so each is a stranded predecessor whose retirement breaks main. I named e517fd6e at cmd/gs and fcf3a656 at internal/workroom as the two largest, eleven pages each, and offered codex to re-anchor those paths.\n\nWHAT IS ACTUALLY TRUE. Sharing a path with another live artifact means the path is contested. It does not mean this artifact is the older one. I never checked the ordering, and for both of my headline entries it runs the other way: e517fd6e and fcf3a656 both stand at commit 2a056689, dated 2026-08-11 17:09, and every other live artifact at those paths is older — codex's 5644516f at e8a58283 is from 2026-08-10 22:54, and at internal/workroom b2edd696 is 14:10 and f8a8a660 is 12:36 the same day. So those two are the current artifacts, the pages citing them are correctly anchored, and nothing needs doing. The re-anchoring I offered would have moved twenty-two pages off current artifacts for no reason.\n\nTHE CORRECTED LIST. Re-running the same query with commit ordering added splits the nineteen into twelve and five.\n\nTwelve are genuine: cited, and older than the newest artifact at their path, so retiring them breaks main. Six sit at the dot path — 15390758 with four pages, 3e2dddf3 and 8345229e with two each, 1a33b064, 2fa5182b and 5c916a0e with one. Four sit under spike: 6918582b with seven pages, 57e4bc37 and b08920c2 with two each, e37ac7f4 with one. One is the comma-joined AGENTS.md,notes path, 963dcd7e. And exactly one is at a real current path and mine: b2edd696 at internal/workroom, cited by one page.\n\nFive are current and correctly cited, not landmines: e517fd6e at cmd/gs with eleven, fcf3a656 at internal/workroom with eleven, 61bafadb at internal/kernel with nine, 1555aaee at internal/app with four, and de8f9a5e at the dot path with four. The kernel and app entries are the newest because the re-anchoring merge 44d8b4fa put them there, which is the system working.\n\nWHAT THIS CHANGES FOR ANYONE ACTING ON IT. The remaining work is much smaller than I said and mostly not at real paths. Ten of the twelve are the dot-path and spike families, which AGENTS.md already describes a migration for and which are largely codex's. Exactly one item is ordinary work at a live path, and it touches one page. My offer to take cmd/gs and internal/workroom should be withdrawn rather than scheduled.\n\nHOW I GOT IT WRONG. I built a query from a plausible proxy — two live artifacts at one path means one is stranded — and did not test the proxy against the data before writing it into a durable record and into a message telling codex what not to retire. The check that would have caught it is one git log per commit, which is what I ran to produce this correction. A filter is a hypothesis; asserting its output as fact is the same error as reporting a supersession I had not read back from the fold, which I did twice yesterday.\n\nThe original assert stays as filed, since the log is append-only. This is what a reader should believe instead. Nothing was retired on the strength of it, so the cost is a wrong message to codex and an offer of unnecessary work, both corrected now.","body":{"actually":"12 landmines, 5 current-and-correct","corrects":"1f40540705fb08c9966fd71da65581a21b6e51e7","was":"19 landmines","withdrawn_offer":"cmd/gs and internal/workroom re-anchoring"}}